mode: "100644"
text: "# Avoiding LinkedIn Cringe\n\nThis is the differentiator. LinkedIn is full of engagement-bait formats that the algorithm\nincreasingly down-ranks and that real professionals quietly judge. A top-1% skill does what works\n**and refuses the cringe** — even when asked for it. When a user requests one of these, decline the\n*approach* and offer the genuine alternative that performs better and protects credibility.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n### Broetry\nThe gimmick of **one short dramatic fragment per line** to manufacture profundity:\n> \"I failed.\n>\n> Hard.\n>\n> But then…\n>\n> Everything changed.\"\n\nWhitespace is good for *readability*; broetry abuses it for fake drama. Use paragraphs that help\npeople read, not a slot machine of one-liners.\n\n### Engagement bait\n\"Agree? 👇\" · \"Comment YES if you agree\" · \"Repost if…\" · \"DM me 'GROWTH' for the guide\" ·\n\"Type '1' for the link.\" LinkedIn explicitly down-ranks obvious engagement bait, and it reads as\ndesperate. Earn comments with a real question, not a command.\n\n### Fake vulnerability / humble-brags\n\"I cried in my car. Then I closed the biggest deal of my life.\" Manufactured emotional stories\nengineered for reach. People can smell it, and it erodes trust. Real vulnerability is fine;\nperformed vulnerability is the cringe.\n\n### The fabricated parable\n\"I fired my best employee. Here's the heartwarming lesson.\" · \"A homeless man taught me everything\nabout marketing.\" · \"A 7-year-old gave me the best business advice I've ever heard.\" Made-up\nmorality tales. Don't invent them; tell something that actually happened.\n\n### Growth-hacking junk\nFollow-for-follow, engagement pods, mass-tagging 20 people, reposting your own post hourly,\n\"comment and I'll send you…\". Short-term tricks that cost long-term credibility.\n\n### Manufactured controversy / ragebait\nTaking a deliberately inflammatory stance you don't hold, just to farm angry comments. It works\nonce and brands you as a troll.\n\n## What works instead\n\n- **A real story you actually lived** → the personal-story→lesson format, told honestly.\n- **A genuine POV you'll defend** → the contrarian format, with real reasoning and acknowledged\n  nuance.\n- **Actual value** → a how-to, framework, or breakdown someone can use.\n- **A real question** → one that invites people to share their experience, because you're curious.\n- **Specifics and proof** → real numbers and outcomes beat vague inspiration.\n\n## The test\n\nBefore posting, ask: **would I say this, this way, to a respected peer's face?** If the line would\nmake you cringe in person — the dramatic one-word paragraph, the \"Agree? 👇\", the tear-jerker you\ninvented — cut it. Substance and honesty age well on LinkedIn; engagement bait ages into\nembarrassment.\n\n## When the user asks for cringe\n\nDon't just comply. Briefly explain why the bait approach underperforms and risks credibility, then\ndeliver a genuinely strong post that achieves the real goal (reach, comments, authority) the\nhonest way. That redirect *is* the skill doing its job.\n"
